.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-JOURNAL-ASSERT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-journal-assert
.Nd prove at every boot that the system journal is recording, and on disk, and repair it when not
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -check
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On a kldload install
.Pa /var/log
is a ZFS dataset of its own, mounted after
.Xr systemd-journald 8
has started.
If journald settles on the wrong place, the machine either keeps no
journal at all while journald reports itself healthy, or keeps one only in
.Pa /run ,
which is erased at every shutdown and capped in size \(em so the boot that
went wrong is exactly the one whose log is gone.
.Pp
.Nm
asks two questions, each by round trip:
.Bl -enum
.It
Does it record?
A unique line is logged and must come back from
.Xr journalctl 1
within ten seconds.
.It
Does it record to disk?
A second unique line must come back reading only
.Pa /var/log/journal
.Pq Ql journalctl -D .
.El
.Pp
If either answer is no it says which, then rotates, flushes and restarts
journald, and asks both again.
It exits 0 when both are yes, and prints a WARNING and exits 1 when the
repair did not make them so.
.Pp
.Pa kldload-journal-flush.service
runs it at every boot, after ZFS has mounted.
It must run as root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -check
Ask both questions and repair nothing: exit 0 when the journal records to
disk, 1 otherwise, saying which.
It creates nothing either, and needs no root.
.Xr kldload-obs-check 8
asks this way.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the description at the top of the script and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_JOURNAL_PROBE_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for a probe line; default 10.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/log/journal/ Ns Ar machine-id Ns Pa /
Where the journal belongs.
.It Pa /run/log/journal/
Where journald keeps it until it is flushed, and loses it at shutdown.
.It Pa /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/persistent.conf
.Ql Storage=auto ,
which removes the race at its source; this tool is the second failsafe.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
The journal records, to disk, possibly after a repair.
.It 1
Still not, after the repair; or, with
.Fl -check ,
not.
.It 2
An unknown argument.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-journal-assert
journal: recording persistently in /var/log/journal (root:systemd-journal)
# (exited 0)
# kldload-journal-assert --check
journal: recording persistently in /var/log/journal (root:systemd-journal)
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
Minutes before, that desktop's journal had been in
.Pa /run
only, since 2026-09-08;
.Ql journalctl --flush ,
run by hand \(em the repair's second step \(em moved it to disk at once.
To check any machine by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# logger -t check "probe-$$"; journalctl -D /var/log/journal -t check | tail -1
# a line ending probe-<pid> means the journal is on disk
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr journalctl 1 ,
.Xr logger 1 ,
.Xr journald.conf 5 ,
.Xr kldload-boot-assert 8 ,
.Xr kldload-obs-check 8 ,
.Xr systemd-journald 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-08-26 a machine recorded nothing across two boots while journald
was active; the first version of this tool checked that a journal file
existed, found a stale one, and never repaired it.
On 2026-08-28 another passed the recording probe while writing only to
.Pa /run .
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: a desktop had kept no journal on disk
for three weeks \(em every boot from 2026-09-08 to 2026-09-28 gone, and
the current one's first nine hours lost to the size cap on
.Pa /run
\(em while this tool reported success at each boot.
Its test for
.Dq on disk
listed the journal files it could read, which included three weeks of old
archives, and taking that list through a pipe it failed on every run in a
way that read as the opposite; after a repair it re-checked only
recording.
Both questions are round trips now, and both are asked again after a
repair.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
A repair restarts journald; the few messages between the probe and the
restart may be the last in
.Pa /run
before it is flushed.
.Pp
Why that desktop's journal stayed in
.Pa /run
from 2026-09-08 is not known: the logs that would say were the ones lost.
A fresh install the same night recorded to disk.
